  "result": "Research | OpenAI (https://openai.com/research/)\nciteturn6556view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://openai.com/research/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 142\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content L1: \nL2:   * [Button: Research]\nL3:   * [Button: Products]\nL4:   * [Button: Business]\nL5:   * [Button: Developers]\nL6:   * [Button: Company]\nL7:   * cite1†Foundation(opens in a new window)†openaifoundation.org L8: \nL9: cite2†Try ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com [Button: Login]\nL10: \nL11: OpenAI\nL12: \nL13: Research\nL14: # Pioneering research on the path to AGI\nL15: \nL16: We believe our research will eventually lead to artificial general intelligence, a system that can solve human-level problems. Our mission is to ensure that AGI benefits all of humanity.\nL17: \nL18: cite3†View research index cite4†Learn about safety L19: \nL20: Focus areas\nL21: \nL22: ### We advance the science of AI through research on frontier models, reasoning, multimodal systems, and safe deployment.\nL23: ### GPT\nL24: \nL25: OpenAI’s GPT series models are fast, versatile, and cost-efficient AI systems designed to understand context, generate content, and reason across text, images, and more.\nL26: \nL27: cite5†Complex coding and professional work at a lower cost ReleaseSep 29, 202612 min read L28: \nL29: cite6†GPT‑6 Astra: A new generation of intelligence ReleaseSep 3, 202628 min read L30: \nL31: cite7†More intelligence from every token for your hardest work ReleaseJul 9, 202618 min read L32: ### Visual\nL33: \nL34: Our research on visual and multimodal AI has led to advances in image generation, vision-language understanding, and models that can reason across images and text.\nL35: \nL36: cite8†Sharper details, faster generation, and more precise editing ChatGPT Images 2.5Sep 8, 20266 min read L37: \nL38: cite9†A state-of-the-art model that makes precise, immediately usable visuals ChatGPT Images 2.0Apr 21, 202611 min read L39: cite10†A natively multimodal model capable of precise, accurate, photorealistic outputs 4o Image GenerationMar 25, 20256 min read L40: ### Audio\nL41: \nL42: Our research on applying AI to audio processing and audio generation has led to advances in speech recognition, real-time speech generation, and models that can listen and respond simultaneously.\nL43: \nL44: cite11†Natural, real-time conversations with AI GPT-LiveJul 8, 202610 min read L45: \nL46: cite12†Navigating the challenges and opportunities of synthetic voices Voice EngineMar 29, 20248 min read L47: \nL48: cite13†Approaching human-level robustness and accuracy on English speech recognition WhisperSep 21, 20222 min read L49: ### Featured roles\nL50: \nL51: We are actively seeking talented individuals to join our team. Explore featured roles or view all open roles.\nL52: \nL53: cite14†View all careers L54: ## Explore all research\nL55: \nL56: cite3†View research index L57: \nL58: Research\nL59: \nL60:   * cite3†Research Index L61:   * cite15†Research Overview L62:   * cite16†Economic Research L63: \nL64: Latest Advancements\nL65: \nL66:   * cite5†GPT-6.1 Sol L67:   * cite6†GPT-6 Astra L68:   * cite7†GPT-5.6 L69:   * cite17†GPT-5.5 L70: \nL71: Safety\nL72: \nL73:   * cite4†Safety Approach L74:   * cite18†Deployment Safety(opens in a new window)†deploymentsafety.openai.com L75:   * cite19†Security & Privacy L76:   * cite20†Trust & Transparency L77: \nL78: Products\nL79:   * cite2†ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L80:   * cite21†ChatGPT Business(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L81:   * cite22†ChatGPT Enterprise(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L82:   * cite23†ChatGPT for Education(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L83:   * cite24†Codex†chatgpt.com L84:   * cite25†Dots(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L85:   * cite26†Release Notes L86: \nL87: API Platform\nL88: \nL89:   * cite27†Overview L90:   * cite28†API Log In(opens in a new window)†platform.openai.com L91:   * cite29†Docs(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L92: \nL93: Business\nL94:   * cite30†Overview L95:   * cite31†Solutions L96:   * cite32†Resources L97:   * cite33†Plugins L98:   * cite34†Customer Stories L99:   * cite35†Partner Network L100:   * cite36†Contact Sales L101: \nL102: Developers\nL103: \nL104:   * cite37†Apps SDK(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L105:   * cite38†Open Models L106:   * cite39†Docs(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L107:   * cite40†Resources(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L108:   * cite41†Developer Forum(opens in a new window)†community.openai.com L109: \nL110: Company\nL111:   * cite42†About Us L112:   * cite43†Our Charter L113:   * cite44†Careers L114:   * cite45†News L115: \nL116: Support\nL117: \nL118:   * cite46†Help Center(opens in a new window)†help.openai.com L119:   * cite47†OpenAI Status(opens in a new window)†status.openai.com L120: \nL121: More\nL122: \nL123:   * cite48†Stories L124:   * cite49†Academy L125:   * cite50†Supply Co. L126:   * cite51†Livestreams L127:   * cite52†Podcast L128:   * cite53†RSS L129: \nL130: Terms & Policies\nL131: \nL132:   * cite54†Terms of Use L133:   * cite55†Privacy Policy L134:   * cite56†Other Policies L135: cite57†(opens in a new window)†x.com cite58†(opens in a new window)†www.youtube.com cite59†(opens in a new window)†www.linkedin.com cite60†(opens in a new window)†github.com cite61†(opens in a new window)†www.instagram.com cite62†(opens in a new window)†www.tiktok.com cite63†(opens in a new window)†discord.gg L136: \nL137: OpenAI © 2015–2026[Button: Manage Cookies]\nL138: \nL139: English United States\nL140: \nL141: [Input: Search]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOpenAI CVE assignment policy | OpenAI (https://openai.com/policies/openai-cve-assignment-policy/)\nciteturn6556view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://openai.com/policies/openai-cve-assignment-policy/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 143\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content L1: \nL2:   * [Button: Research]\nL3:   * [Button: Products]\nL4:   * [Button: Business]\nL5:   * [Button: Developers]\nL6:   * [Button: Company]\nL7:   * cite1†Foundation(opens in a new window)†openaifoundation.org L8: \nL9: cite2†Try ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com [Button: Login]\nL10: \nL11: OpenAI\nL12: \nL13: November 20, 2025\nL14: # OpenAI CVE assignment policy\nL15: \nL16: Scope\nL17: \nL18:   * cite3†Scope L19:   * cite4†Acknowledgment and communication L20:   * cite5†CVE ID assignment and tracking L21:   * cite6†Researcher recognition and rewards L22: \nL23:   * cite3†Scope L24:   * cite4†Acknowledgment and communication L25:   * cite5†CVE ID assignment and tracking L26:   * cite6†Researcher recognition and rewards L27: OpenAI is a CVE Numbering Authority (CNA) for vulnerabilities in our products and services. We assign CVE IDs for security issues reported by external parties in OpenAI systems and publish CVE Records to the cite7†public CVE list⁠(opens in a new window)†trust.openai.com . We are committed to a transparent, cooperative disclosure process that keeps our users safe while giving credit to the researchers who help keep our platforms secure.\nL28: ## Scope\nL29: \nL30: This policy covers technical security vulnerabilities discovered outside OpenAI’s standard internal security processes in OpenAI products that could reasonably compromise the confidentiality, integrity, or availability of OpenAI systems, software we distribute, and data.\nL31: AI model safety vulnerabilities which include behavior or content (prompt “jailbreaks,” model hallucinations, policy bypasses, etc) are not within scope of this policy. We encourage reporting AI safety issues through dedicated cite8†channels .\nL32: \nL33: To report a vulnerability in our systems, please see our cite9†Coordinated vulnerability disclosure policy .\nL34: ##\nL35: Acknowledgment and communication\nL36: \nL37: Initial response: We will aim to acknowledge receipt of vulnerability reports within 3 business days.\nL38: \nL39: Assessment and triage: Our security team will investigate the report to validate the vulnerability and determine its impact and severity. During this phase, we may contact you to request additional information.\nL40: \nL41: Updates: We will share information about the progress of the investigation when there is noteworthy information to share, as appropriate.\nL42: Disclosure: While we are working on a fix, we ask that you do not share or publicize the vulnerability details. OpenAI likewise will treat your report as confidential. We won’t disclose your report outside of OpenAI and relevant partners, consistent with our cite10†Outbound Coordinated Vulnerability Disclosure policy . Keeping the vulnerability non-public can help protect our users and systems until a solution or sufficient mitigation is in place.\nL43: Once a solution or sufficient mitigation is in place, we will work with you to coordinate a disclosure timeline.\nL44: ## CVE ID assignment and tracking\nL45: \nL46: Assigning a CVE ID helps us and the community track the issue across our communications and ensures it’s recognized in global vulnerability databases.\nL47:   * Scope for assigning a CVE ID: We will generally assign a CVE ID for security vulnerabilities in software that we distribute, including those that require user action to resolve. We will generally not reserve a CVE ID for server-side issues. We will only assign a CVE ID for security vulnerabilities that are exploitable and that are important security issues. We will not assign a CVE ID for defense-in-depth fixes, misconfigurations, nor informational findings.\nL48:   * When we assign the CVE ID: In many cases, we will reserve a CVE ID early in the process, once we’ve confirmed the issue and begun working on a resolution. Once a CVE ID is reserved for the vulnerability, we’ll share that ID with you. The CVE ID remains in a reserved (not yet public) state before public disclosure.\nL49:   * OpenAI disclosure: We will maintain a disclosure page with confirmed, public CVEs on OpenAIs Trust Portal at cite11†trust.openai.com⁠(opens in a new window)†trust.openai.com . Public CVE ID entries will link to related advisories published by OpenAI.\nL50: ## Researcher recognition and rewards\nL51: \nL52: We appreciate the contributions of security researchers.\nL53: \nL54:   * Public acknowledgment: If requested, we will credit the reporter (by name or alias, and/or affiliation) in any published advisory or release notes, as well as in the CVE disclosure page.\nL55: \nL56:   * Bug bounty rewards: If a report was submitted through our bug bounty program, the reporter may be eligible for a monetary reward, subject to our published rules and discretion.\nL57: Thank you for helping keep OpenAI and our users safe. We view the relationship with the security community as a collaboration. By following this policy and working with us in good faith, you’re making a real difference. We’re committed to reciprocating that good faith by being responsive, fair, and appreciative of your efforts. If you have any questions about these guidelines or need clarification on our CNA process, feel free to reach out at disclosure@openai.com.\nL58: Let’s continue to work together to protect and secure the technology that’s shaping our future!\nL59: Research\nL60: \nL61:   * cite12†Research Index L62:   * cite13†Research Overview L63:   * cite14†Economic Research L64: \nL65: Latest Advancements\nL66: \nL67:   * cite15†GPT-6.1 Sol L68:   * cite16†GPT-6 Astra L69:   * cite17†GPT-5.6 L70:   * cite18†GPT-5.5 L71: \nL72: Safety\nL73: \nL74:   * cite19†Safety Approach L75:   * cite20†Deployment Safety(opens in a new window)†deploymentsafety.openai.com L76:   * cite21†Security & Privacy L77:   * cite22†Trust & Transparency L78: \nL79: Products\nL80:   * cite2†ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L81:   * cite23†ChatGPT Business(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L82:   * cite24†ChatGPT Enterprise(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L83:   * cite25†ChatGPT for Education(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L84:   * cite26†Codex†chatgpt.com L85:   * cite27†Dots(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L86:   * cite28†Release Notes L87: \nL88: API Platform\nL89: \nL90:   * cite29†Overview L91:   * cite30†API Log In(opens in a new window)†platform.openai.com L92:   * cite31†Docs(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L93: \nL94: Business\nL95:   * cite32†Overview L96:   * cite33†Solutions L97:   * cite34†Resources L98:   * cite35†Plugins L99:   * cite36†Customer Stories L100:   * cite37†Partner Network L101:   * cite38†Contact Sales L102: \nL103: Developers\nL104: \nL105:   * cite39†Apps SDK(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L106:   * cite40†Open Models L107:   * cite41†Docs(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L108:   * cite42†Resources(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L109:   * cite43†Developer Forum(opens in a new window)†community.openai.com L110: \nL111: Company\nL112:   * cite44†About Us L113:   * cite45†Our Charter L114:   * cite46†Careers L115:   * cite47†News L116: \nL117: Support\nL118: \nL119:   * cite48†Help Center(opens in a new window)†help.openai.com L120:   * cite49†OpenAI Status(opens in a new window)†status.openai.com L121: \nL122: More\nL123: \nL124:   * cite50†Stories L125:   * cite51†Academy L126:   * cite52†Supply Co. L127:   * cite53†Livestreams L128:   * cite54†Podcast L129:   * cite55†RSS L130: \nL131: Terms & Policies\nL132: \nL133:   * cite56†Terms of Use L134:   * cite57†Privacy Policy L135:   * cite58†Other Policies L136: cite59†(opens in a new window)†x.com cite60†(opens in a new window)†www.youtube.com cite61†(opens in a new window)†www.linkedin.com cite62†(opens in a new window)†github.com cite63†(opens in a new window)†www.instagram.com cite64†(opens in a new window)†www.tiktok.com cite65†(opens in a new window)†discord.gg L137: \nL138: OpenAI © 2015–2026[Button: Manage Cookies]\nL139: \nL140: English United States\nL141: \nL142: [Input: Search]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nResearch \\ Anthropic (https://www.anthropic.com/research)\nciteturn6556view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.anthropic.com/research\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 56\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content cite1†Skip to footer L1: \nL2:   * Research\nL3:   * cite2†Policy L4:   * Commitments\nL5:   * Learn\nL6:   * cite3†News L7: \nL8: cite4†Try Claude†claude.ai L9: \nL10: # Research\nL11: \nL12: Our research teams investigate the safety, inner workings, and societal impacts of AI models—so that artificial intelligence has a positive impact as it becomes increasingly capable.\nL13: \nL14: Research teams:cite5†Alignment cite6†Economics cite7†Interpretability cite8†Societal Impacts cite9†Frontier Red Team L15: ### Alignment\nL16: \nL17: The Alignment team works to understand the risks of AI models and develop ways to ensure that future ones remain helpful, honest, and harmless.\nL18: \nL19: ### Economics\nL20: \nL21: The Economics team studies how AI is reshaping the economy, including work, productivity, and economic opportunity.\nL22: \nL23: ### Frontier Red Team\nL24: \nL25: The Frontier Red Team analyzes the implications of frontier AI models for cybersecurity, biosecurity, and autonomous systems.\nL26: ### Interpretability\nL27: \nL28: The mission of the Interpretability team is to understand how large language models work internally, as a foundation for AI safety and positive outcomes.\nL29: ### Societal Impacts\nL30: \nL31: Working closely with the Anthropic Policy and Safeguards teams, Societal Impacts is a technical research team that explores how AI is used in the real world.\nL32: \nL33: cite10†Image: Scenarios for our Economic Future L34: cite11†Scenarios for our Economic Future Anthropic’s Economics team is sharing a new model of how AI may affect economic growth, jobs, wages, and more by 2030. The model lets you explore the scenarios, tell us what you think will happen, and see how your answers compare to +10,000 Americans. L35: cite12†ScienceOct 8, 2026 The missing map of the sky Brice Ménard, an astrophysicist at Johns Hopkins University and a researcher at Anthropic, explains how he worked with Claude Science to produce the first complete map of the sky in UV light. cite13†Societal ImpactsSep 29, 2026 What do you want from AI? We’re launching a new study using Anthropic Interviewer to learn from your experiences with AI. cite14†EconomicsSep 24, 2026 Project Swap: What happens when agents trade for us?To see what works and what breaks when agents are sent into a market, we made a miniature market of Claudes. cite15†Sep 17, 2026 Measurements for understanding the pace of AI development inside frontier labs Today, the world can’t see what’s going on inside AI labs.\nL36: Anthropic is proposing new metrics that would give the public visibility into frontier AI development. L37: ## Publications\nL38: \nL39: Search[Input: Search]\nL40: \nL41: Date Category Title\nL42:   * cite16†Oct 12, 2026Frontier Red Team AI models may be capable of self-replicating without access to a datacenter L43:   * cite17†Oct 12, 2026Policy Strengthening our defenses against distillation attacks L44:   * cite18†Oct 9, 2026Alignment Investigating unintended model actions in our evaluations and internal use L45:   * cite12†Oct 8, 2026Science The missing map of the sky L46:   * cite19†Oct 8, 2026Frontier Red Team Launching an opt-in vulnerability-finding service for open-source software L47:   * cite20†Oct 1, 2026Science Claude-shaped science L48:   * cite21†Sep 30, 2026Economics What work can robots do? L49:   * cite13†Sep 29, 2026Societal Impacts What do you want from AI? L50:   * cite22†Sep 29, 2026Frontier Red Team GLM-5.3 and the spread of advanced cyber capabilities L51:   * cite23†Sep 25, 2026Science Yes, Claude can do Nine Loops L52: cite24†See more L53: Join the Research team\nL54: \nL55: cite25†See open roles "
